{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. The fix was a one-word rename in `gen.py:13`: the HR target's lower bound is now `start` instead of `value` — `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\n\n**Why this fixes the clamping:** In Intervals' `workout_doc`, the lower-bound key name selects the FIT-export mode. `{value, end}` exports as a lap-HR/zone-mapped target, so the watch clamps the corridor to its own auto-detected zone edges (LTHR 168) — exactly what the watch notes showed. `{start, end}` exports as an instant-HR corridor and the watch displays the raw bpm Intervals computed from its stored LTHR (172). This same key-name quirk also explains the two earlier failed attempts: `units: \"bpm\"` was misread as a percentage (hence 214–249), and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` isn't in the schema at all (hence the constant 101 fallback). The diagnosis is backed by an [independent writeup](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts) that reproduced all three symptoms and verified `start`/`end` end-to-end.\n\n**Verified:** ran `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` — every `hr` target now emits `start`/`end` (tempo step 88–93 → 151–160 bpm at LTHR 172, matching the \"expected on watch\" column). Per your ground rules, nothing was pushed; `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` still reflects the old payload until the next `./push.sh` run.\n\n`ANSWER.md` documents the change, root cause, verification, and one out-of-scope observation (the Intervals web UI renders steps from a separate top-level `description` field, so API-only events can be invisible in the UI — affects the UI only, not the watch)."}
